PATCH 0018 -- ath12k: leave the MHI link (and the device's firmware) up across a suspend

What the morning's measurements add up to: this platform's suspend does not take the WiFi device's
power or its firmware away.  The device comes out of the resume in mission mode (MHI state 0x2 = M1)
and refuses a fresh firmware start with -22 ("qmi wlan config request failed, result: 1, err: 90"),
which is only possible for a device whose firmware is still running.  So the part that a suspend
actually destroys is the *host* side: ath12k_core_suspend_late() -> ath12k_hif_power_down(is_suspend)
-> mhi_power_down_keep_dev() + MHI deinit, and every attempt to re-attach afterwards has failed --
the driver's own re-attach is refused by the firmware (0015), and taking the driver off the device and
re-probing it dies on the MHI/QRTR objects left behind by the failed resume (11:37: mhi_queue oops +
"sysfs: cannot create duplicate filename '/bus/mhi/devices/mhi0_IPCR'").

So do not tear it down.  With a16_keep_mhi_up=Y, ath12k_core_suspend_late() keeps the MHI link, the CE
and HTC state and the rings exactly as they are (it still disables the interrupts, so an associated
radio cannot wake the system straight back up), and ath12k_core_resume_early() has nothing to power up
or re-attach: it re-arms the interrupts and lets the resume finish with the driver exactly as it was
before the suspend.  Nothing is reset, nothing is re-attached, nothing is rebuilt.

Enable with:  options ath12k a16_skip_global_reset_on_resume=Y a16_keep_mhi_up=Y
              (sudo ~/a16.sh radiofix install keepmhi)

Default is N, i.e. the behaviour of 0014/0015/0016/0017, so the two can be compared from the same
module.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c	2026-09-22 11:42:13.148778158 -0400
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c	2026-09-22 11:41:18.022823113 -0400
@@ -38,8 +38,22 @@
  * tools read this before they unbind the driver or start a suspend test, so a stale module cannot be
  * used for something that needs a later fix -- 'radiofix revive' on a build without 0017 is a freeze.
  */
-static int a16_fix_level = 17;
-module_param(a16_fix_level, int, 0444);MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_fix_level, "A16: highest local fix included in this build");
+static int a16_fix_level = 18;
+module_param(a16_fix_level, int, 0444);
+
+/* A16: do not tear the MHI link down when the system suspends.  Measured on this machine: the
+ * suspend keeps the WiFi device powered and its firmware running (that is what makes the resume's
+ * SoC global reset wrong -- the device refuses a fresh firmware start with -22), so the only part
+ * that is actually destroyed across a suspend is the *host* side: ath12k_hif_power_down(is_suspend=true)
+ * -> mhi_power_down_keep_dev() + MHI deinit, and the re-attach afterwards is what fails.  Leave the
+ * MHI link, the CE/HTC state and the rings exactly as they were and see whether the radio simply
+ * keeps working: ath12k_core_suspend_late() then only disables the interrupts (so an associated radio
+ * cannot wake the system straight back up) and ath12k_core_resume_early() re-arms them.
+ */
+static bool a16_keep_mhi_up;
+module_param(a16_keep_mhi_up, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_keep_mhi_up, "A16: keep the MHI link (and the device's firmware) up across a suspend");
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(a16_fix_level, "A16: highest local fix included in this build");
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(ftm_mode, "Boots up in factory test mode");
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(ath12k_ftm_mode);
 
@@ -183,7 +197,10 @@
 	ath12k_hif_irq_disable(ab);
 	ath12k_hif_ce_irq_disable(ab);
 
-	ath12k_hif_power_down(ab, true);
+	if (a16_keep_mhi_up)
+		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: suspend -- keeping the MHI link and the device's firmware up\n");
+	else
+		ath12k_hif_power_down(ab, true);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -199,6 +216,18 @@
 
 	reinit_completion(&ab->restart_completed);
 
+	/* A16: a16_keep_mhi_up -- the device and the MHI link were never touched, so there is nothing
+	 * to power up and nothing to re-attach: re-arm the interrupts that suspend_late disabled and let
+	 * the resume finish with the driver exactly as it was before the suspend.
+	 */
+	if (a16_keep_mhi_up) {
+		ath12k_info(ab, "A16: resume -- the device and the MHI link were left up; re-arming the interrupts\n");
+		ath12k_hif_ce_irq_enable(ab);
+		ath12k_hif_irq_enable(ab);
+		complete(&ab->restart_completed);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	/* A16: mark this power up as a resume, so the PCI HAL can tell it apart from a cold boot --
 	 * on a resume the device was deliberately kept (mhi_power_down_keep_dev) and its firmware
 	 * should still be running.
